uence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum for $1\\le n\\le 10$ is ${s}_{n}=2n.$ In particular, ${s}_{10}=2\\cdot 10=20.$\n\nLet's compute the first few partial sums for $n\\ge 11:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{11} & =20+0=20 \\\\ {s}_{12} & =20+0=20 \\\\ {s}_{13} & =20+0=20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the sequence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum for $n\\ge 11$ is\n\n$$\n{s}_{n}=20.\n$$\n\nThe sequence of partial sums is convergent:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }20=20\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converges to $20.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186666137 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3{y}_{1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}3(-1)^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3(0)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\